Q: Making a javascript string sql friendly A: <p>It turns out that mysql_real_escape_string() is pretty trivial, as is its replacement <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/mysqli.real-escape-string.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>mysqli::real_escape_string</code></a>. <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.mysql-real-escape-string.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">According to the documentation</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>mysql_real_escape_string()</strong> calls MySQL's library function mysql_real_escape_string, which prepends backslashes to the following characters: \x00, \n, \r, , ', &quot; and \x1a.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sounds pretty simple, actually. You could do something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function mysql_real_escape_string (str) {
    return str.replace(/[\0\x08\x09\x1a\n\r&quot;'\\\%]/g, function (char) {
        switch (char) {
            case &quot;\0&quot;:
                return &quot;\\0&quot;;
            case &quot;\x08&quot;:
                return &quot;\\b&quot;;
            case &quot;\x09&quot;:
                return &quot;\\t&quot;;
            case &quot;\x1a&quot;:
                return &quot;\\z&quot;;
            case &quot;\n&quot;:
                return &quot;\\n&quot;;
            case &quot;\r&quot;:
                return &quot;\\r&quot;;
            case &quot;\&quot;&quot;:
            case &quot;'&quot;:
            case &quot;\\&quot;:
            case &quot;%&quot;:
                return &quot;\\&quot;+char; // prepends a backslash to backslash, percent,
                                  // and double/single quotes
            default:
                return char;
        }
    });
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: I haven't run this through any sort of unit test or security test, but it does seem to work -- and, just as an added bonus, it escapes tabs, backspaces, and '%' so it can also be used in LIKE queries, as per <a href="https://www.owasp.org/index.php/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet#MySQL_Escaping" rel="nofollow noreferrer">OWASP's recommendations</a> (unlike the PHP original).</p>
<p>I do know that <code>mysql_real_escape_string()</code> is character-set-aware, but I'm not sure what benefit that adds.</p>
<p>There's a good discussion of these issues <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/229889/sanitizing-mysql-user-parameters">over here</a>.</p>

Q: Pass variable to custom component A: <p>You need to add <code>Input</code> property to your component and then use property binding to pass value to it:</p>
<pre><code>import { Component, Input, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'my-custom-component',
    templateUrl: './my-custom-component.html',
    styleUrls: ['./my-custom-component.css']
})
export class MyCustomComponent implements OnInit {

    @Input()
    customTitle: string;

    constructor() {
        console.log('myCustomComponent');
    }

    ngOnInit() {
        console.log(this.customTitle);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And in your template:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;my-custom-component [customTitle]=&quot;yourVariable&quot;&gt;&lt;/my-custom-component&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>For more info, check out <a href="https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/cookbook/component-communication.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this page</a>.</p>

Q: How do I solve `malloc(): corrupted top size`? A: <p>For starters there are numerous memory leaks at least due to the function <strong>cleanLine</strong></p>
<pre><code>char* cleanLine(char* d, FILE* fp) {
    char* c = strdup(d);
    //....
    return c;
}
</code></pre>
<p>where there is used function <strong>strdup</strong> that dynamically allocates memory that is not freed in the program. Moreover the address of the allocated memory can be lost.</p>
<p>And the address of the initial value of the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> that also points to dynamically allocated memory also is lost due to this statement and moreover can be equal to <strong>NULL.</strong></p>
<pre><code>instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>As a result such calls as that</p>
<pre><code>fgets(instruction, 100, fp) 
</code></pre>
<p>or using the pointer further in the program invoke undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Look at this <strong>while</strong> loop within the function <strong>cleanLine</strong></p>
<pre><code>char* cleanLine(char* d, FILE* fp) {
    char* c = strdup(d);
    while (*c == ' ' || *c == '/' || *c == '\n') {
        if (*c == ' ') c++;
        if (*c == '/' || *c == '\n') {
            if (fgets(c, 100, fp) == NULL) return NULL;
        }
    }
    //...
</code></pre>
<p>The pointer <strong>c</strong> can be incremented within it if there are present leading spaces in the string. Thus this call of <strong>fgets</strong></p>
<pre><code>if (fgets(c, 100, fp) == NULL) return NULL;
</code></pre>
<p>will try to access and possibly overwrite memory outside the dynamically allocated string that by the way can be initially less than <strong>100</strong> bytes because it is a copy of another string. That again invokes undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Looking at the code I do not see a reason why you are allocating memory dynamically for a character array where you are reading records from the file. It would be much simpler and safer to declare a character array</p>
<pre><code>char instruction[100];
</code></pre>
<p>Also separating calls of <strong>fgets</strong> between <strong>main</strong> and  the function <strong>cleanLine</strong> is a bad design. It would be much better to write a function that returns to <strong>main</strong> a next command or <strong>NULL</strong> from the file. At present within the while loop</p>
<pre><code> while (fgets(instruction, 100, fp) != NULL) {
     instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> can be a <strong>NULL</strong> pointer returned by the function <strong>cleanLine</strong>.</p>
<p>And the function <strong>instructionType</strong></p>
<pre><code>int instructionType(char* c) {
    if (*c == '(') return TYPE_L;
    else if (*c == '@') return TYPE_A;
    else return TYPE_C;
}
</code></pre>
<p>can also invoke undefined behavior if the value of the first character of the passed string is not one of the listed values in the if-else statements. Instead of these macros</p>
<pre><code>#define TYPE_A 0
#define TYPE_C 1
#define TYPE_L 2
</code></pre>
<p>It is better to declare an enumeration adding one more element that signals a bad command.</p>
<p>And also you need to check whether the file was opened successfully.</p>
<p>So my advice is redesign your program entirely.</p>
<p>For example you could define a function named like <strong>getNextInstruction</strong> the following way</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;
#incluse &lt;string.h&gt;

const size_t N = 100;

char * getNextInstruction( char *buffer, FILE *fp )
{
    char comment = '/';
    bool found = false;
    char *first = NULL;

    while ( !found &amp;&amp; fgets( buffer, N, fp ) != NULL )
    {
        size_t n = strcspn( buffer, &quot;\n&quot; );
        buffer[ n ] = '\0'; 

        first = buffer;

        while ( isblank( ( unsigned char )*first ) ) ++first;

        if ( *first != '\0' &amp;&amp; *first != comment )
        {
            char *last = buffer + n;

            while ( isblank( ( unsigned char )last[-1] ) ) --last;

            *last = '\0';

            found = true;
        }
    }

    return found ? first : NULL;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And in <strong>main</strong> you need to declare a pointer that will accept a returned value from the function.</p>
<p>For example</p>
<pre><code>char buffer[N];
char *instruction;

while ( ( instruction = getNextInstruction( buffer, fp ) ) != NULL )
{
    // check the instruction and do something else if required
    // as for example to call strdup for the string 
    // pointed to by instruction 
}
</code></pre>
<p>And at last some words relative to other two functions.</p>
<p>Within the function <strong>initSymbol</strong></p>
<pre><code>SYMBOLS** initSymbol(int capacity, int* totalCount) {
    SYMBOLS** table = malloc(sizeof(SYMBOLS*) * capacity);
    return table;
}
</code></pre>
<p>the parameter <strong>totalCount</strong> is not used.</p>
<p>The function <strong>addEntry</strong> does not check whether memory was allocated successfully. It should return either <strong>true</strong> or <strong>false</strong> depending on the result.</p>
<p>And it would be good to declare one more structure that contains the array of pointers, its capacity and the current number of actual elements in the array.</p>

Q: C# Parse String into DateTime with Specific TimeZone A: <p>Here is a function that should do it:</p>
<pre><code>DateTimeOffset ConvertToCentralTime(string dateStr)
{
    var dt = DateTime.ParseExact(dateStr, &quot;yyyyMMddHHmm&quot;, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    TimeZoneInfo centralZone = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById(&quot;America/Chicago&quot;);

    // convert to utc time with implied central time zone
    DateTime utcDt = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeToUtc(dt, centralZone);

    // covert from utc back to central
    var centralDt = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(new DateTimeOffset(utcDt), centralZone);

    return centralDt;
}

// outputs 3/13/2015 3:57:00 PM -05:00
Console.WriteLine(ConvertToCentralTime(&quot;201503131557&quot;));
</code></pre>

Q: Subscribe event not getting called everytime A: <p>Instead of passing <code>this</code> as a parameter to the <code>subscribe</code> method, why don't you bind that anonymous function with <code>this</code> object? Like this:</p>
<p><strong>JS Code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>this.GraphClick.subscribe(function (val) {
  model.showGraphResults(true);
  model.GraphIntervalSelected(val);
  searchSpeechGraphView(speechUsage);
}.bind(this));
</code></pre>
